{:db/id 17592186092385 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id 17592186091954 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/id #uuid "df59a07a-922c-50b9-8c2c-22ed57ac2bda" :question/math-academy-id "e-149" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x) = 2\\sqrt{x} -4x$ at the point $(1,-2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}-4x) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}-4x) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2}-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n     \n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm=f'(1) = (1)^{-1/2} - 4 = -3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\nm' = -\\dfrac{1}{m} = \\dfrac 1 3.\n$$\n\nThe equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y+2 & =\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{1}{3}-2 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{3}x-\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{3}x & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(7,0).$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id 17592186049394}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id 17592186092507 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17dbb435-0cbf-53b0-b774-95a09e5dd5e2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(-1,0)" :db/id 17592186241384} {:answer/id #uuid "52de3f96-3021-5e86-a521-dd5eaf2b4c3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id 17592186241918} {:answer/id #uuid "7aaf9455-fc42-5674-a140-8638e322d2c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(5,0)" :db/id 17592186242258} {:answer/id #uuid "8b234896-b4c5-57ed-a626-2274af12bce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16,0)" :db/id 17592186242411} {:answer/id #uuid "aecf5e6b-c2d8-5faf-a09d-6ece73a85a07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{16}{5},0)" :db/id 17592186242704}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b234896-b4c5-57ed-a626-2274af12bce9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(16,0)" :db/id 17592186242411} :answer-field/id #uuid "562461af-5947-5911-b5fb-04453b8765df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186243609}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a8debb9f-1a0f-5757-8884-2cca273ae3ea" :question/math-academy-id "q-10871" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2{x}^{2}+x$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{2}+x)=4x+1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=4(1)+1=5,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =-\\frac{1}{5}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{1}{5}+3 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{5}x+\\frac{16}{5} & =0 \\\\ x & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(16,0).$"} {:db/id 17592186092510 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "142b6536-fed4-529f-ac1d-8a42fc666730" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,1)" :db/id 17592186244498} {:answer/id #uuid "65112d5b-e5af-58b2-8b42-2fafe84f3bf1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,2)" :db/id 17592186245208} {:answer/id #uuid "6e29c4b1-5a97-5a97-bbf2-51eabed39e9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{5}{2})" :db/id 17592186245289} {:answer/id #uuid "ca93f7a3-eace-5053-ae98-706828a1b4af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{1}{2})" :db/id 17592186246111} {:answer/id #uuid "d23fbfd4-1cbd-54d1-aa24-886860426aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id 17592186246184}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d23fbfd4-1cbd-54d1-aa24-886860426aba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,3)" :db/id 17592186246184} :answer-field/id #uuid "816b14ca-c9ce-5b90-8b9c-06306f5bacc2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186246773}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "24c7ef87-44ff-5aaa-8c80-717e24087d16" :question/math-academy-id "q-11124" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{x}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{1/2}) \\\\  & ={x}^{-1/2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-1(x-1) \\\\ y & =-x+1+2 \\\\ y & =-x+3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-0+3 \\\\ y & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,3).$"} {:db/id 17592186233120 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "73d11496-6181-52ca-9d4d-b27d18c44afc" :question/math-academy-id "q-10878" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y={x}^{2}$ at the point $(3,9)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2})=2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(3)=2(3)=6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =-\\frac{1}{6}(x-3) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{1}{2}+9 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{6}x+\\frac{19}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{19}{2} & =\\frac{1}{6}x \\\\ x & =57.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(57,0).$"} {:db/id 17592186233928 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "cbb07659-6477-5d10-82e6-aacdbe268de0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186232870} {:answer-field/id #uuid "8c274c86-2265-560b-954c-b88302a2ca2b" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186232871}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2fa5d441-4b5d-54d5-8541-9752428a4a54" :question/math-academy-id "q-342222" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=2\\sqrt[4]{x}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt[4]{x}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{1/4}) \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{4}{x}^{-3/4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{x}^{3}}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(1)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt[4]{{1}^{3}}}=\\frac{1}{2},\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(\\frac{1}{2})}=-2.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-2\\cdot (x-1) \\\\ y & =-2x+2+2 \\\\ y & =-2x+4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2x+4 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =4 \\\\ x & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,2,0\\,).$"} {:db/id 17592186234308 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5576e6ce-2a23-51d7-abb9-62b7ddb98d68" :question/math-academy-id "q-71542" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3{x}^{2}-4x+1$ at the point $(2,5)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{'}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-4x+1)=6x-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{'}(2)=6(2)-4=8,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{'}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =-\\frac{1}{8}(x-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{1}{4}+5 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{8}x+\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-\\frac{1}{8}\\cdot 0+\\frac{21}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{21}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\frac{21}{4}).$"} {:db/id 17592186240795 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1f5ae20c-b789-5249-bff8-1194daf3c008" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-20)" :db/id 17592186238321} {:answer/id #uuid "bdc902fd-c7c3-53b3-90a3-02661fb40990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,20)" :db/id 17592186239711} {:answer/id #uuid "d1c92a83-db3b-57d5-87ad-2844ddc86d19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-8)" :db/id 17592186239896} {:answer/id #uuid "ee996a51-be22-5564-9782-5ded740f8f13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,4)" :db/id 17592186240158} {:answer/id #uuid "f14cea86-7dde-5527-ac9c-2767c9873574" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-4)" :db/id 17592186240186}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bdc902fd-c7c3-53b3-90a3-02661fb40990" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,20)" :db/id 17592186239711} :answer-field/id #uuid "092d888b-3d25-51dd-8dad-08178716d6c0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240342}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9348de04-831f-5ba3-963b-71f2ffb24276" :question/math-academy-id "q-104073" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}$ at the point $(8,12)$ intersects the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2/3}) \\\\  & =2{x}^{-1/3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(8)=\\frac{2}{\\sqrt[3]{8}}=1,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-1.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-12 & =-(x-8) \\\\ y & =-x+8+12 \\\\ y & =20-x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $y$-axis by setting $x=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =20-0 \\\\ y & =20.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $y$-axis at  $(0,20).$"} {:db/id 17592186240796 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2aec5b68-893e-5d74-9a97-1f1d78197fe8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(3,0)" :db/id 17592186238434} {:answer/id #uuid "39be9afa-fb3a-514b-a31f-626575d486e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{7},0)" :db/id 17592186238553} {:answer/id #uuid "659ab06c-c705-55e3-b1cd-020841118552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},0)" :db/id 17592186238943} {:answer/id #uuid "81f08a0b-8c19-5ecf-80ec-e6d88e15c8ff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(1,0)" :db/id 17592186239184} {:answer/id #uuid "a3667d4f-7c52-56e0-aa34-3236afb88174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id 17592186239478}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3667d4f-7c52-56e0-aa34-3236afb88174" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(7,0)" :db/id 17592186239478} :answer-field/id #uuid "f02d54c7-4347-5d6c-bb82-af5e4a1dca44" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id 17592186240752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4c51a1e-c57a-5c02-97b1-4b0a0b84934b" :question/math-academy-id "q-104075" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $f(x)=2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}$ at the point $(1,2)$ intersects the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{3/2}) \\\\  & =3{x}^{1/2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=3\\sqrt{1}=3,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-2 & =-\\frac{1}{3}(x-1) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{1}{3}+2 \\\\ y & =-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{1}{3}x+\\frac{7}{3} & =0 \\\\ x & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at  $(7,0).$"} {:db/id 17592186297306 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c44b5185-3568-53b3-8d77-c69fc2155067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186296303}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c44b5185-3568-53b3-8d77-c69fc2155067" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186296303} :answer-field/id #uuid "26be343c-5dfd-5031-b3d8-1563d7798319" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186296704} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55838d61-7b48-5ee6-9d1d-e85a07554987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186295760}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55838d61-7b48-5ee6-9d1d-e85a07554987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186295760} :answer-field/id #uuid "f48c190e-f08c-555e-ba42-c4bbbf078a30" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186297131}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ac03aacc-0b7e-5a7f-8554-ee3b669a879c" :question/math-academy-id "q-308823" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=\\frac{2}{x}+1$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{2}{x}+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(2{x}^{-1}+1) \\\\  & =-2{x}^{-2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{{x}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-\\frac{2}{(1)^{2}}=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the  $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$"} {:db/id 17592186317215 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f29a7ee8-6004-5b63-95be-61dae16a9261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186316470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f29a7ee8-6004-5b63-95be-61dae16a9261" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id 17592186316470} :answer-field/id #uuid "a37137d9-0f3c-517e-a020-3af32e0d7118" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186316927} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "956a031d-532f-5607-903a-8423c21b005b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186315946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "956a031d-532f-5607-903a-8423c21b005b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186315946} :answer-field/id #uuid "ab3b85e2-94cd-5534-8e57-152046c2fe00" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186316946}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "13b0b6f2-9e09-5a5a-a8f6-564d0221d479" :question/math-academy-id "q-342200" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=4-{x}^{2}$ at the point $(1,3)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(4-{x}^{2})=-2x.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(1)=-2(1)=-2,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-2)}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{2}(x-1) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x-\\frac{1}{2}+3 \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}x+\\frac{5}{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}x & =-\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ x & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-5,0\\,).$"} {:db/id 17592186317216 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3ba61258-92a9-511e-891f-9e6adce2edb0" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb0d502b-d760-5a8c-9578-c9ee4ec4fcff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id 17592186316175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb0d502b-d760-5a8c-9578-c9ee4ec4fcff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-30" :db/id 17592186316175} :answer-field/id #uuid "6e1ebef9-6d7b-5c01-bb3d-23418a788f7c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186316814} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20941452-3ae7-5d6b-8a37-42ad9a21730b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186315301}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "20941452-3ae7-5d6b-8a37-42ad9a21730b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id 17592186315301} :answer-field/id #uuid "ddf70354-1c4a-52c1-8859-6faef41810f9" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id 17592186317077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2f7f3a21-7e8d-51e9-8d45-2915b0e3fe06" :question/math-academy-id "q-342201" :question/problem "Find where the normal line to the curve $y=3{x}^{2}-6x+5$ at the point $(0,5)$ intersects the $x$-axis.\n\nThe normal line intersects the $x$-axis at\n$($\n{{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}} $).$" :question/worked-solution "Taking the derivative, we have\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(3{x}^{2}-6x+5)=6x-6.\n$$\n\nSo the slope of the tangent line is\n\n$$\nm={f}^{′}(0)=6(0)-6=-6,\n$$\n\nand therefore the slope of the normal line is\n\n$$\n{m}^{′}=-\\frac{1}{m}=-\\frac{1}{(-6)}=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the equation of the normal line is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =\\frac{1}{6}(x-0) \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{6}x+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLastly, we find the point of intersection with the $x$-axis by setting $y=0$ and solving the resulting equation. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{6}x+5 & =0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{6}x & =-5 \\\\ x & =-30.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the normal line intersects the $x$-axis at $(\\,-30,0\\,).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Intercepts of a Normal Line"}
